<main id="main">
  <section class="hero ink">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="kicker">Editorial standards</p>
      <h1>How this site is written, and what it will not claim.</h1>
      <p class="lead">A market full of unpublished figures rewards confidence. We would rather be checkable. These are the rules we hold ourselves to, published so you can hold us to them.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section mist">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h2 class="reveal">Where a figure comes from</h2>
      <ul class="checklist reveal">
        <li><strong>Primary source first</strong>A buyer&rsquo;s own page, filing or announcement, linked where we can link it.</li>
        <li><strong>Third-party collations are labelled</strong>If a number came from someone else&rsquo;s summary of a buyer&rsquo;s page, the page says so, and the summariser is named.</li>
        <li><strong>Every figure carries a date</strong>Ranges change. A number without a date is a rumour with a dollar sign.</li>
        <li><strong>We say what kind of number it is</strong>An advertised band, a calculator output, a worked example and a disclosed licence are four different kinds of evidence, and we never present one as another.</li>
        <li><strong>No composite figures</strong>We do not average unrelated published numbers to produce a market rate. If such an average exists anywhere, it is not on this site.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section teal-soft">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h2 class="reveal">What we do not publish</h2>
      <ul class="checklist reveal">
        <li><strong>Funding figures we cannot reconcile</strong>Where a company states three different totals in three places, we state none.</li>
        <li><strong>A price for your dataset</strong>Nobody can price a corpus before seeing it. A number quoted before diligence is a sales tactic.</li>
        <li><strong>Results we have not had</strong>Our own ledger starts when the first deal closes. Until then the page says so.</li>
        <li><strong>Testimonials we do not have</strong>No invented quotes, no borrowed logos, no &ldquo;as seen in&rdquo;.</li>
        <li><strong>Audit claims</strong>We are not certified, audited or accredited by anyone. If that changes, it will be stated with the certificate.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="reveal">The rule underneath all five: if a sentence would be embarrassing to defend in front of a buyer&rsquo;s counsel, it does not go on the page.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section mist">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h2 class="reveal">How we use AI</h2>
      <p class="reveal">Some drafts on this site begin with machine assistance. The rules are narrow.</p>
      <ul class="checklist reveal">
        <li><strong>AI is never a source</strong>Every factual claim is checked against the document it cites, not against a model&rsquo;s summary of it.</li>
        <li><strong>Every figure is traced</strong>A number that cannot be traced to a page we can quote does not appear.</li>
        <li><strong>A person signs off</strong>Each page is reviewed before publication, and reviewed again when it changes.</li>
        <li><strong>Dates reflect the review</strong>The reviewed date on a page is the date a person last checked it, not the date a draft was generated.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section teal-soft">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h2 class="reveal">Corrections</h2>
      <p class="reveal">If something here is wrong, tell us through the <a href="contact.html">contact form</a> and say which page and which sentence. We will check it against the source and correct it.</p>
      <p class="reveal">Corrections are noted on the page with the date, and we do not quietly rewrite a claim that has been quoted elsewhere. A page that was wrong and is now right is more useful than a page that pretends it was never wrong.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section mist">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h2 class="reveal">Who pays for this site</h2>
      <p class="reveal">DataLeasers is paid by the data buyer when a company it introduces signs a licence. Sellers pay nothing for the introduction. That is a commercial interest, and it is disclosed on every page that discusses fees.</p>
      <p class="reveal">It also shapes what we can say. We do not publish a price we cannot source, we do not promise an outcome, and we publish the buyer&rsquo;s terms including the ones that are bad for a seller, such as 24-month exclusivity. A broker that recommends only its own buyer has nothing to compare.</p>
